what:
fastcompile-queue 1: the daemon reads and writes the plane history section through `blaze_history::section` (items 00735/00738).

`crates/blaze-history/src/section.rs` now owns the section: `SCHEMA` (the same string as `serve/image.rs` `HISTORY`, so existing images still read), `write(&History, &mut Builder)`, `restore(&Image<S>, Config) -> Result<History, HistoryError>`, and `HistoryError::starts_fresh()` (true for `Refused` and `Decode(DecodeError::Version)`, false for `Corrupt` and other decode errors).

In `crates/blaze-cargo/src/serve/image.rs`:
- Delete `pub const HISTORY` and use `blaze_history::section::SCHEMA` in its tests (`with_history(HISTORY, ..)`).
- `history` becomes:
```rust
pub fn history<S: Stable>(image: &Image<S>, config: blaze_history::Config) -> Result<History, String> {
    match blaze_history::section::restore(image, config) {
        Ok(history) => Ok(history),
        Err(error) if error.starts_fresh() => {
            eprintln!("blaze serve: {error}; the plane's history starts empty");
            Ok(History::new(config))
        }
        Err(error) => Err(error.to_string()),
    }
}
```
- Line 146 `builder.add(SectionId::PLANE_HISTORY, HISTORY, &parts.predict.encode());` becomes `blaze_history::section::write(parts.predict, builder);` (with the builder in scope as there).
- Drop the now-unused `DecodeError` and `SchemaHash` imports if nothing else uses them.

Why: one owner per section layout; today `blaze_graph::history` and the daemon each name a schema for one section ID. Once this lands, `crates/blaze-graph/src/history.rs` (the `Estimates` form, unused) can be deleted by its owner.
